# Differential-testing finding (issue #317): a Turtle IRIREF ("<...>") whose
# non-ASCII bytes are UTF-8-encoded Unicode characters is double-encoded by
# Factoidal's Turtle parser -- the resulting IRI is corrupted (mojibake),
# and the corruption is VALID UTF-8 (so it looks fine to `file`/naive
# byte-count checks; only decoding it reveals the wrong characters).
# TriG has the identical bug (shares the same IRIREF scanner). N-Triples
# parsing of an IRIREF with the SAME raw bytes is correct.
#
# Repro:
#   bin/linux-x86_64/factoidal canonicalize tools/difftest/repros/turtle_iriref_unicode_mojibake.ttl
#   # Factoidal: "<https://example.org/æ¥æ¬èª> ..." (WRONG -- corrupted IRI)
#   # pyoxigraph / rdflib (format=turtle): "<https://example.org/日本語> ..." (correct)
#
# See docs/designissues/2026-07-29-differential-testing-ledger.md, finding 2.
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/onto#> .
<https://example.org/日本語> ex:p "plain literal café" .
